      <package fileName="rc-astro-cli_v1.1.0.zip" sha1="d3765035bcb502f6329d27bdb01c6d0a2f4c659e" type="script" releaseDate="20260928">
         <title>RC-Astro CLI Wrappers</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>RC-Astro CLI Wrappers v1.1.0</b>
            </p>
            <p>Runs BlurXTerminator, StarXTerminator and NoiseXTerminator through the stand-alone RC-Astro command-line tool instead of the compiled PixInsight plug-ins. Adds Script &gt; RC-Astro &gt; BlurXTerminator / StarXTerminator / NoiseXTerminator (CLI).</p>
            <p>Why: on Blackwell GPUs (RTX 50-series, compute capability 12.0) the plug-ins&apos; bundled TensorFlow has no sm_120 kernels — it JIT-compiles every kernel from PTX, exhausts system RAM into swap, and aborts. The RC-Astro CLI uses ONNX Runtime instead and runs roughly 2.7 s per 22 MP panel on GPU.</p>
            <p>BlurX and NoiseX modify the target view in place (undoable). StarX writes &lt;id&gt;_starless, plus &lt;id&gt;_stars when enabled, to new windows and leaves the original untouched; the astrometric solution is carried over. Full dialogs, saveable process icons, and view-target automation for headless pipelines.</p>
            <p>REQUIRES the separately-purchased RC-Astro CLI (rc-astro) version 2.0 or newer on your PATH (update an older CLI with rc-astro update --install, then run the install.sh it downloads), with BlurXTerminator / StarXTerminator / NoiseXTerminator licensed and their models downloaded. These scripts bundle no RC-Astro code — they shell out to it, and report a clear error if it is not installed. GPU acceleration additionally needs system cuDNN 9.13 or newer; older cuDNN silently falls back to CPU on Blackwell.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="gaia-depth-grade_v1.0.12.zip" sha1="d4c4c6850290cb86648694ca73be2d637ca9e420" type="script" releaseDate="20260928">
         <title>Gaia Depth Grade</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>Gaia Depth Grade v1.0.12</b>
            </p>
            <p>Physically-grounded depth grade for astrophotographs using Gaia distances. Defaults to the offline local Gaia DR3 database (the one PixInsight uses for plate-solving); an online Bailer-Jones option is available. On first run it fetches a frozen Python sidecar (~95 MB, linux-x64) from GitHub Releases and verifies it by SHA-256. Requires StarXTerminator.</p>
            <p>v1.0.12 (sidecar 1.0.7): enlarged (near) stars now wear a realistic STAR-SHINE HALO. The halo&apos;s radial shape is stacked from the frame&apos;s own bright, unsaturated, isolated stars and tinted to each star&apos;s own colour, with amplitude anchored to its real peak brightness — replacing the achromatic Gaussian blob that read as fake. Frames with too few clean reference stars fall back to a Moffat profile (with a warning). First run re-fetches the ~95 MB sidecar.</p>
            <p>v1.0.11 (sidecar 1.0.6): adds optional NEBULA DEPTH. A new checkbox grades the starless layer too — dark dust/globules come forward, the diffuse glow recedes, with gentle atmospheric perspective and noise-cored structural relief. The spatial variation comes from the nebula&apos;s own structure; the overall strength is calibrated by the Gaia matched-star distance spread, so the nebula depth matches the stars. Opt-in (off by default) with live Nebula depth / atmospheric / structure sliders.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
   </platform>
</xri>
